# Untitled

The Impact of Secondary Building Units in Metal–Organic Frameworks on Plasmonic Gold-Sensitized Photocatalysis
Embedding plasmonic metals in metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) can build an advanced visible-light photocatalyst architecture utilizing the localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) effect, while the practical performances have been restricted by the sluggish charge transfer at metal–MOF interface and through the secondary building units (SBUs) of the adopted carboxylate MOFs currently. Herein, a pyrazolate Ni-MOF (PFC-9) featured with an 1D SBU chain is selected to be the host catalyst to immobilize Au nanoparticles as a novel and optimized construction for LSPR photocatalysis. Compared with the common 3D-connected SBUs of carboxyl- \(\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{ZrO}_{\mathbf{x}}\) and pyrazole- \(\cdot\mathsf{N i O}_{\mathbf{x}}\) configurations in the reference MOFs, the 1D \((-\mathsf{N i}-\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{p z}}-\mathsf{N}_{\mathsf{p z}}-\mathsf{I}_{\infty}\) chain in PFC-9 creates abundant Au/MOF contacts, a short and low-resistant pathway for Au-to- \(\cdot\mathsf{N i}^{2+}\) transport of hot electrons, and enables fluent electron utilization at the continuous active Ni sites. Consequently, the Au/PFC-9 photocatalyst achieves the optimum activity for visible-light-driven \(\mathsf{H}_{2}\) production. This work shows an example to promote the LSPR-sensitized photocatalysis taking the advantage of MOFs' structural tunability, providing significant guidance for the rational design of highly efficient photocatalysts.
## 1. Introduction
Producing clean and renewable chemical energy by the solar photocatalysis technique is of great promise to solve the global energy and environmental crises.[1] The efficiencies of photocatalysis are generally determined by the charge generation in light-harvesting units, the charge separation and transfer toward catalytic sites, and the redox reaction of substrate molecules
with electrons/holes at these sites. Plasmonic metals (e.g., nanosized Au, Ag, and Cu) can efficiently harvest visible light by the localized surface plasmon resonance (LSPR) effect and transmit the photo-induced hot electrons or the resonance energy to the adjacent catalysts.[2] Thus, loading plasmonic metals on semiconductors, the well-studied photocatalytic materials, has been proved to be a valid strategy to achieve enhanced catalytic activities under wide-range incident light.[2b,c,3] However, the limited surface area of the host semiconductors usually leads to low loading amounts or self-aggregation of the plasmonic metals, both of which decrease the LSPR efficiencies. Moreover, the lack of abundant active sites on pristine semiconductors would cause delayed utilization of the transmitted electron or energy from LSPR metals. Namely, seeking for host materials with high flexibility on structural tuning is highly
desired for the optimization of LSPR-sensitized photocatalysis.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs), built by metal ion/cluster nodes and polydentate organic ligands in long-range order, is an emerging class of porous crystalline materials with inherent ultrahigh surface areas that would facilitate the highamount loading of plasmonic metals.[4] In addition, the orderly micro- or mesopores of MOFs could confine the loaded metal to suitable sizes for LSPR, and the component tunability accompanied with structural accuracy allows precisely customizing the anchoring sites to immobilize plasmonic metals.[5] Owing to these advanced properties, significant progress has been made when adopting MOFs as host materials for LSPR-driven photocatalysis.[2b,3c,6] However, to retain structure stability in photocatalytic conditions, most of the adopted MOFs are constructed by the coordination of O-donor carboxylate ligands and high-valent metal oxide cluster such as \(\mathrm{Zr^{IV}O_{x}},\) forming widebandgap secondary building units (SBUs) with sluggish charge transfer through the carboxyl- \(\cdot\mathrm{ZrO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) interfaces and depressed charge utilization at metal nodes.[7] To efficiently utilize the LSPR converted solar energy for catalysis, it is still urgent to further design and explore new host MOFs with more advanced charge-transfer kinetics.
Recent studies have shown that N-donor ligands with pyrazole/triazole as chelating groups enable strong coordination with low-valent reactive metals such as \(\mathrm{{Ni}(I I)}\) species to build
robust MOFs in harsh conditions.[4b,8] Our group has further demonstrated the ultrafast electron transfer at the pyrazole-Ni coordination interfaces in MOF achieving boosted photocatalysis compared with the carboxylate-based analogs.[8] Therefore, instead of the commonly-used carboxylate MOFs, the pyrazole-based Ni-MOFs would be promising hosts to immobilize plasmonic metals toward optimized LSPR photocatalysis. To date, none of such incorporations has been reported yet and the expected optimization of LSPR charge kinetics remains unconfirmed.
Herein, we selected PFC-9 (PFC = Porous materials from FJTRSM CAS), composed of pyrazole (Pz) ligands and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{3+}\) ion nodes forming an 1D \((- \mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}^{-}})_{\infty}\) coordination chain, as the host MOF to embed Au nanoparticles (NPs). Compared with the routine SBUs consisting of carboxyl groups and isolated \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) clusters, the PZ groups at PFC-9 were found to assist the initial anchoring of \(\mathrm{Au}^{3+}\) precursor and induce a close location of \(\mathrm{Au}^{0}\) NPs toward the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) nodes. Notably, such pyrazolate SBUs with a 1D \((- \mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}^{-}})_{\infty}\) chain minimizes the resistance of hot-electron transfer from plasmonic Au to the catalytically active Ni node as well as the subsequent electron transport throughout the continuous Ni cites in PFC-9, which is superior to the charge dynamics in analogous MOFs built by 3D-connected SBUs with carboxyl- \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) and even \(\mathrm{Pz}-\mathrm{NiO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) configurations. As a result, \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) composites achieves the optimum LSPR-sensitized photocatalysis for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production under visible light irradiation. This work demonstrates a capable model for constructing optimized LSPR metal/MOF composite materials via the molecule-level adjustments in the compositions and connecting manner of SBUs in MOFs, paving a new way for the design of highly efficient photocatalysts.
## 2. Results and Discussion
PFC-9 was fabricated according to our previous report.[4b] The reaction between 1,4-Di(1H-pyrazol-4yl)benzene \(\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{BDP}\right)\) and \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) ions form an infinite 1D coordination chain as \((- \mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}^{-}})_{\infty}\) and scaffolds rhombic 1D channels in structure (Figure 1a). Powder x-ray diffraction (PXRD) analysis and \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) sorption isotherm confirm the phase purity and microporosity of the as-synthesized PFC-9 with the desired crystal
structure and large Brunauer–Emmett–Teller (BET) surface area \((1216\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\), respectively (Figure 1b,c). Then the loading of Au species into PFC-9 was conducted in water via a two-step operation including the initial absorption of \(\mathrm{AuCl}_{4}^{-}\)and subsequent reduction by \(\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}\) (See details in supporting information). The crystallinity of PFC-9 remains unchanged after the loading of Au as verified by PXRD patterns. Although no obvious diffractions of Au species were observed (Figure 1b; Figure S1a, Supporting Information), the inductively coupled plasma-optical emission spectroscopy (ICP-OES) reveals Au loading amount of \(1.9\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) within the \(5\,\mathrm{wt\%}\) Au/PFC-9 sample (herein after referred to as Au/PFC-9, Table S1, Supporting Information). Furthermore, the \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) uptake and the pore volume \((0.569\,\mathrm{cm}^{3}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) as well as the calculated BET surface area \((527\,\mathrm{m}^{2}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\) are apparently lower than ones of the bare MOF, indicating the occupancy of Au species in the cavities of PFC-9 via the in situ growth.
More evidence has been obtained from transmission electron microscope (TEM) images. While the scanning electron microscope (SEM) image of Au/PFC-9 shows no obvious Au particles on the surface of PFC-9 agglomerates (Figure 2a), the high-angle annular dark-field scanning TEM (HAADF-STEM) image and the corresponding energy dispersive x-ray (EDX) mapping demonstrate a uniform dispersity of Au element throughout the MOF substrate as shown in Figure S2 (Supporting Information). In the detailed TEM visions, these distinct particles feature a size distribution ranging from 1.7 to \(7\,\mathrm{nm}\) with a majority at \(\approx4.5\,\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure 2b) and can be further identified as Au NPs by the clear lattice fringe of \(0.236\,\mathrm{nm}\) corresponding to the Au (111) plane in the high-resolution TEM image of a chosen representative (Figure 2c).[9] Additionally, a specific HAADF-STEM inspection, showing nanoscale Au aggregates inlaid in Ni-featured substrate (Figure 2d–f), also confirms the identification of Au NPs and their dispersity within PFC-9 as revealed above. It is worth to mention that, comparing with Au/PFC-9, operating the in situ Au loading procedure with the same precursor dose \((5\,\mathrm{wt\%}\,\mathrm{K}_{\mathrm{AuCl}_{4}})\) on the commonly adopted UiO-66, built by isolated carboxyl- \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) SBUs, results in lower content of immobilized Au NPs \((1.5\,\mathrm{wt\%}\), Table S1, Supporting Information), whereas their particle sizes are large enough to be observed on MOF surfaces readily via SEM (Figure S3, Supporting Information), which suggests a higher-level aggregation of the limited
Au NPs in the composite. This contrast together with the above results verifies the advantageous Au loading in porous PFC-9 achieving a balance between high contents and uniform dispersion.
To disclose the atomic-level interfacial circumstance within Au/PFC-9, X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was employed for detailed analysis. By examining the possible change of XPS peaks for each element during the in situ Au loading process represented by Figure 3a, the initial and final binding sites for Au species are supposed to be revealed. As shown in Figure 3b, Au 4f signal of the \(\mathrm{KAuCl_{4}/P F C-9}\) sample (PFC-9) absorbing \([\mathrm{AuCl_{4}}]^{-}\) before chemical reduction can be well-fitted to two sets of double peaks assigned to \(\mathrm{Au}^{3+}\) \((4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) at \(87.1~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}\) at \(90.4~\mathrm{eV}\) ) and \(\mathrm{Au}^{0}\) \((4\mathrm{f}_{7/2}\) at \(85.1~\mathrm{eV}\) and \(4\mathrm{f}_{5/2}\) at \(88.8~\mathrm{eV}\) ), respectively, announcing an \(\mathrm{Au}^{3+}\)-to- \(\mathrm{Au}^{0}\) reduction after the initial loading step.[10] Meanwhile, a new N 1s peak arises at \(400.9~\mathrm{eV}\) beyond the original one at \(399.8~\mathrm{eV}\) for \(\mathrm{KAuCl_{4}/P F C-9}\) (Figure 3c), indicating the electron loss in parts of the \(\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}\) in PFC-9 during \([\mathrm{AuCl_{4}}]^{-}\) loading. It is worth mentioning that the lone pair electrons of non-metallic atoms in the lattice can be exploited to bond with a single \(\mathrm{AuCl_{x}}\) complex and induce a spontaneous Au reduction, echoing the situation of Au 4f and N 1s here.[11] Hence, the new N 1s peak at \(400.9~\mathrm{eV}\) is attributed to the formation of \(\mathrm{Au-N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}\) bonds. After the further chemical reduction by \(\mathrm{NaBH_{4}}\), Au species have been completely reduced to \(\mathrm{Au}^{0}\), and the new N 1s peak survives but exhibits
a decreased intensity and a negative shift to \(400.7~\mathrm{eV}\). This conforms to the expected in situ growth of Au NPs in PFC-9, during which partial Au atoms fell off the original \(\mathrm{Au-N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}\) coordination and assembled at the adjacent maintained sites to form nanosized \(\mathrm{Au}^{0}\) particles. And due to the less electron sharing to \(\mathrm{Au}^{0}\) than to \(\mathrm{Au}^{3+}\), the binding energy of the new N 1s peak decreases. Therefore, the initial and final binding sites for Au NPs in PFC-9 are attributed to the \(\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}\) atoms in the structure. Such a close location of Au to the \((- \mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}})-\infty\) chain of PFC-9 also induces a charge interaction between Au and the \(\mathrm{Ni}^{0}\) nodes, as evidenced in Figure 3d that the binding of \(\mathrm{Au}^{3+}\) causes a positive shift of Ni \(2\mathrm{p}_{3/2}\) from \(855.7\) to \(855.9~\mathrm{eV}\) owing to an electron-withdrawing effect \((\mathrm{KAuCl_{4}/P F C-9})\) while the final Au reduction leads to its negative shift back to \(855.6~\mathrm{eV}\) (Au/PFC-9), which may be beneficial to the future catalysis.[10a]
After the structural determination, the photophysical and photochemical properties of this composite were then evaluated. First, the light response of Au/PFC-9 that correlates to the charge generation steps was inspected. As shown in Figure 4a, pristine PFC-9 displays light absorbance up to \(\lambda=600~\mathrm{nm}\), while its effective photocurrent response ends at \(\approx500~\mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S4, Supporting Information). With loading of Au NPs (1.9 wt%, Table S1, Supporting Information), the light absorption in the full spectrum of PFC-9 as well as the \(\lambda\)-resolved photocurrent intensities are entirely enhanced (Figure 4a,b), which is ascribed to the efficient light scattering
of Au NPs in PFC-9. Although no obvious Au LSPR absorption peak was observed in the \(\lambda\) range of \(500{-}600\ \mathrm{nm}\), an evident photocurrent response in this region notably emerged, demonstrating the existence of the well-known electron injection via the LSPR effect from Au NPs (Figure 4b). It is presumed
that the inconspicuous LSPR absorption peak in diffuse reflectance spectra (DRS) could be a consequence of the small sizes and uniform distribution of Au NPs in PFC-9. Similarly, when loading a close amount of Au NPs \((2.0\,\mathrm{\wt}\%)\) on PFC-6 constructed by the same pyrazolate ligand, the LSPR absorption is
also hidden. On the contrary, less-content but larger-sized Au NPs supported by the carboxylate UiO-66 induce an apparent LSPR band in region of \(\lambda\,{=}\,500{-}600\ \mathrm{nm}\) (Figure S5, Supporting Information). Despite the intensity of LSPR band, the large sizes of Au NPs may reduce their contact interface with UiO-66 compared with the pyrazolate MOFs. This echoes the advantage of pyrazolate SBUs for homogeneous Au loadings in MOF.
Since MOFs with 1D SBU chain and azolate ligands have been reported to exhibit pronounced metal-ligand orbital overlap and energy matching that are favorable for conductivity, PFC-9 with 1D \(\left(-\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}-\right)_{\infty}\) chain was expected to reveal good charge mobility.11 Thus, the electron transfer behaviors between Au NPs and PFC-9 were investigated. As shown in Figure S6 (Supporting Information), bare PFC-9 in the dark condition exhibits an EPR signal at \(g=2.003\), the characteristic symbol of free electrons, which is intensified by the following light irradiation. That means either these nonequilibrium electrons induced by inevitable crystal defects or the photo-excitation are delocalized or free throughout PFC-9 rather than trapped at some particular sites, conforming to the function of 1D \(\left(-\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}-\right)_{\infty}\) chain mentioned above. Moreover, this EPR signal is further enhanced in Au/PFC-9 under light irradiation (Figure 4c), indicating that the LSPR hot electron from Au NPs can also freely transfer through the \(\left(-\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{Pz}}-\right)_{\infty}\) chain within PFC-9.
To acquire more understanding of the facilitated charge transfer by the advanced SBUs in PFC-9, further comparisons with UiO-66 and PFC-6 scaffolded by 3D-connected isolated SBUs (Figure 5a) were conducted with electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS). In Figure 5b, these bare MOFs show the order of arc radius as \(\mathrm{UiO-66}>\mathrm{PFC-6}>\mathrm{PFC-9}\), which suggests the most expedited charge transfer process within PFC-9
and also the advantage of pyrazolate SBUs over the carboxylate analogues. The EIS arc radiuses are further reduced with the incorporation of Au NPs in these MOFs in that Au/PFC-9 exhibits the shortest arc (Figure 5c), corroborating the beneficial charge transportation in Au/PFC-9 by its appressed and abundant Au/SBUs interfaces. Thus, the Au/PFC-9 with the LSPR light harvesting and the advanced interfacial charge transfer shows the most intensive photocurrent among these Au/MOFs composites under visible light irradiation (Figure 5d).
Encouraged by such superior charge-transfer kinetics of Au/PFC-9, we examined the performances for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production with a series of comparisons under visible light irradiation (Figure S7, Supporting Information). With a negative conduction band potential, PFC-9 is able to catalyze the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution when receiving the LSPR hot electrons from Au NPs (Figure S8, Supporting Information). As expected, under visible light irradiation embedding Au NPs in PFC-9 greatly enhances the photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) yields (Figure 6a), asserting the key role of the supported Au in the improvement. The highest activity has been reached by the \(5\ \mathrm{wt\%}\) Au/PFC-9 sample \((130\,\upmu\mathrm{mol}\,\mathrm{h}^{-1}\,\mathrm{g}^{-1})\), which is over 17 times higher than that of bare PFC-9 \((7.5\ \upmu\mathrm{mol}\ \mathrm{h}^{-1}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1}\), Figure S9, Supporting Information). Moreover, the apparent quantum yields (AQY) of Au/PFC-9, calculated from the \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) yields under monochromatic light irradiations, are found to range along with its light absorbance except for an arresting enhancement at \(\approx\!\lambda\,=\,600\,\mathrm{~nm}\) (Figure 6b; Table S2, Supporting Information), which is in good accordance with the trend of wavelength-dependent photocurrents above owing to the LSPR effects of Au NPs (Figure 4b). To further clarify the essential role of the LSPR effect on photocatalysis, Pt and Pd metals were chosen for comparison
because of their LSPR response mainly in UV light region,[13] i.e., their negligible LSPR strength above \(\lambda\,{>}\,400\;\mathrm{nm}\) . As shown in Figure 6c, Pt/PFC-9 and Pd/PFC-9, prepared via the same procedure with the metal precursor percentages close to that of \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) , produce less \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) gas than does \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) in the same photocatalytic condition. With the fact that Pt and Pd are usually more active than Au for catalyzing \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution reaction (HER), the contrast indicates that the outstanding photocatalytic activity of \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) should be principally contributed by the LSPR photosensitization of Au NPs in visible light region, rather than the role as HER catalyst. It is worth mentioning that, in most of the LSPR-photosensitized MOF systems, cocatalysts especially Pt NPs are inevitable to approach high activities of photocatalytic HER due to the unqualified catalysis of metal nodes. Thanks to the catalytically active \(\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) nodes in the \((-\mathrm{Ni-N_{Pz}-N_{Pz}-})_{\infty}\) chain, the \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) here performs high activities without any additional cocatalysts, saving the material cost, which is even comparable to the all-inorganic photocatalyst systems (Table S3, Supporting Information). To reveal the necessity of the 1D chain SBUs in PFC-9 for photocatalytic \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) evolution, a comparison was made among \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) , \(\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) , and \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-6}\) with an approximate Au loading mass \((\approx\!2.0\;\mathrm{wt}\%)\) . The \(\mathrm{N}_{2}\) -sorption results manifest that both \(\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) and \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-6}\) possess higher BET surface area than does \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) , and that the pore sizes of the three samples are large enough for the mass transport of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}\) and triethanolamine molecules, i.e., all the pore surface is accessible
to the reactants (Figures S10 and S11, Supporting Information). However, as displayed in Figure 6d, \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) shows a higher rate of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production than both \(\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) and \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-6}\) . This contrast excludes the insignificant influence from the unequal surface area on the LSPR-sensitized photocatalytic performance and uncovers the unique impact from the diverse SBUs of these three MOFs. Thus, considering the HER rate order of \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}>\mathrm{Au/PFC-6}>\mathrm{Au}/\mathrm{UiO}{-}66\) , both the compositions and connecting manners of the 1D \((-\mathrm{Ni-N_{Pz}-N_{Pz}-})_{\infty}\) SBUs should be indispensable to achieve abundant \(\mathrm{Au/MOF}\) interfaces with expedited hot-electron transfer and subsequent utilization as revealed above, namely, be responsible for the superior photocatalysis of \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) . Additionally, \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) performs a durability for at least 5 runs of cycled photocatalysis (Figure 6e) and maintained the crystallinity and morphology (Figure 6f; Figure S12, Supporting Information), which is favorable for future practical usage.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, a uniform distribution of plasmonic Au NPs in MOF was achieved via the anchoring by \(\mathrm{Pz}\) groups in the SBUs of PFC-9, creating abundant \(\mathrm{Au/MOF}\) interfaces and short Au-to- \(\cdot\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}\) node distances. The LSPR-induced hot electrons can be easily received to PFC-9 via the low-resistant \(\mathrm{Pz-Ni}^{2+}\) coordination in SBUs and freely
transport throughout the 1D \(\left(-\mathrm{Ni}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{PZ}}-\mathrm{N}_{\mathrm{PZ}}-\right)_{\infty}\) chain for photo-reduction. Consequently, the promoted charge transfer dynamics and the optimum photocatalytic activities for \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\) production were achieved by \(\mathrm{Au/PFC-9}\) composite, superior to the cases loading Au on MOFs built by 3D-connected SBUs with carboxyl- \(\mathrm{ZrO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) or \(\mathrm{Pz}–\mathrm{NiO}_{\mathrm{x}}\) coordination. This work echoes the structural tunability of MOFs to enhance the LSPR photocatalysis, capable of the rational design of highly efficient photocatalysts.
